EldenNet, the read-only round (package @LABEL@)
@UNDERLINE@

This package runs a read-only round of EldenNet's address library (Lodestar; EldenNet's decisions O100 to O102) on
your own PC. The two offline runs you made read the game's executable file and found the 217 places where the game
keeps its main objects (EldenNet calls them managers: one keeps the world's characters, another the time of day, and
so on), without knowing which is which. This round starts the game with EldenNet loaded and reads, from
the game as it runs, which of those places holds which of the eleven managers EldenNet needs. Then it tries, for a few
minutes, the way EldenNet v1 will read the game: from a thread of its own, about ten times a second.

This is the seventh such round. The first six (22 November) named ten of the eleven, at the same places each time,
and read them without a fault; they found where the play time is kept, and the sixth wrote that place into its table.
Still missing is the time of day. The sixth round looked for it in the object behind every one of the known places and
found nothing that behaves as minutes and hours do; but that test could not have seen a clock whose minutes go by
faster than one in three seconds. So this round uses a surer sign, and needs your help for it: when you let time pass
at a grace, the clock jumps. The round looks for a number that rises steadily while you play and jumps exactly then.

It also follows the game's event flags two steps from where they start, and goes through the memory there for single
bits that change once: a flag set by something you do. For that it asks you to do one or two things the game remembers
for good (step 3 says which).

Two faults of the sixth round are mended. Its report grew past its size limit after seven minutes, and the round then
left out every detail of what it watched, the event flags' among them; now it writes less to begin with, and when the
report is still too large it leaves out one object's detail at a time, the largest first, and says which. And a slow
rise was written too coarsely to judge; it is written a thousand times finer now.

It has no wide search of the game's memory: that one made the game stutter in the fourth round and stays off. What is
new is much smaller and aimed: up to 2 MiB of memory from each of at most eight addresses the event flags' own object
keeps, and up to 256 KiB from each of at most 32 addresses one step further (about 24 MiB in all), read a piece at a
time at the same careful pace as the sixth round's reads, a pass every thirty seconds at most. If the game stutters
all the same, quit it and tell us.

It only reads. It calls nothing in the game, adds nothing to it and changes nothing in it. There is no key to press.
Nothing in this package is for playing with yet.

Built from EldenNet's engine branch at commit @COMMIT@ with clang-cl (Release). Never shared: a test build for you
alone, labelled @LABEL@.


How long it takes
-----------------

About 25 minutes: 5 to unzip and start, 1 to 2 at the title screen, about 15 in the world with your save loaded, and a
few to send the files. The order and the pauses of step 3 matter more than the exact minutes. Longer does no harm.


What is in it
-------------

  read.cmd                Double-click this to start the round.
  eldennet-launcher.exe   EldenNet's launcher: backs up your saves, starts the game offline without Easy Anti-Cheat,
                          loads EldenNet, and watches the game until it ends.
  eldennet.dll            EldenNet in the game: the probe build, which runs this round and nothing else.
  eldennet-engine.exe     EldenNet's command-line tool, here to check the report (its lint command).
  gal\                    What the round works from: the build's fingerprint (builds.toml), how a manager's place is
                          recognised (recipes.toml, with its note in derivations\), EldenNet's own numbers and labels
                          for the places it needs (ids.toml), and what to look for (leads.toml). Numbers and names of
                          the design's own, nothing taken from the game.
  README-READ.txt         This file.
  SHA256SUMS              The SHA-256 of every other file in this package.
  LICENSE.txt             EldenNet's licence (MIT).
  THIRD_PARTY.md          What EldenNet is built with, and its credits.
  THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt The licences of the libraries inside the programs.


Before you start
----------------

  - Close the game, and start Steam.
  - Unzip this package into a folder of its own, anywhere but the game's folder.
  - The launcher backs up your saves before it starts the game (in %APPDATA%\EldenNet\save-backups), and the round
    does not run without that backup. If you want a copy of your own as well, copy the folder %APPDATA%\EldenRing
    somewhere first.
  - The game will run offline, without Easy Anti-Cheat. Use a save you are happy to play offline, and never take a save
    played with mods into online play.
  - The launcher's messages appear in a console window behind the game. Setting the game to windowed mode first makes
    them easy to read; otherwise press Alt+Tab to look, and Alt+Tab back.
  - Windows or your security software may warn about an unsigned program. Allow it only if the SHA-256 values match
    (below); otherwise stop and tell us.


The steps
---------

1. Double-click read.cmd. (Or, in a command prompt in this folder: eldennet-launcher.exe --read)

   The first time, the launcher asks where eldenring.exe is if it cannot find it through Steam, and shows its notices.

2. The game starts. At the title screen, wait. The console says, in lines starting with "EldenNet read:", that it is
   reading the game. This takes about a minute. Do not load a save until it tells you to.

3. The console then says "Now load your save." with how many of the eleven managers it has named so far (some exist
   only once a save is loaded), or, if all eleven are there already, "All 11 managers are named. If you have not
   loaded your save yet, load it now." Either way, load your save. Then, in the world, in this order (a watch or a
   phone's clock beside you helps; a minute more or less does not matter):

     a. For the first five minutes, walk about: go near an NPC or an enemy, and open the map once. Do not rest at a
        grace yet. The round needs this time to learn how fast each number rises.
     b. After about five minutes, rest at a grace and let time pass until the middle of the day. Get up.
     c. Walk about for three minutes. Then rest at a grace again (the same one will do) and let time pass until
        night. Get up.
     d. Walk about for two minutes. Then do one thing the game remembers for good, for example: pick up an item
        from a body or a chest that you have not taken yet, open a door or a chest that stays open, discover a
        grace, or defeat an enemy that does not come back. If nothing of the kind is at hand, buy one thing from a
        merchant who has only a few of it.
     e. Two minutes later, do a second such thing if one is at hand. If not, never mind.
     f. Walk about for two more minutes.

   If your grace does not offer to let time pass, skip b and c, do the rest, and tell us.

   Please note roughly when you did b, c, d and e (minutes after loading the save) and what d and e were, and tell
   us with the files: "b at 5, c at 8, d at 10: opened a chest" is all we need.

4. Then quit the game as you usually do, whatever the console has said. The console then says whether the two files
   passed their check, and the launcher ends.

5. Send these files back to us:

     %APPDATA%\EldenNet\probe\lodestar-found.json   required
     %APPDATA%\EldenNet\probe\lodestar-table.toml   required
     %APPDATA%\EldenNet\logs\eldennet.log           helpful
     %APPDATA%\EldenNet\logs\launcher.log           helpful
     %APPDATA%\EldenNet\crashes\                    the newest file there, only if the game crashed

   (Type %APPDATA%\EldenNet into Explorer's address bar to open the folder.) You can check the report yourself:

     eldennet-engine.exe lint --report "%APPDATA%\EldenNet\probe\lodestar-found.json"

   It must say "lint: the report is clean". The launcher has checked it the same way already.

If anything else happens (the console says the build is not supported, or that the round stopped, or the game closes),
do not try again: send the two logs and tell us what the console said. That is a useful result too.


What it does
------------

  - Backs up your saves (it only copies them), checks that Steam is running and that the game is not, and checks that
    your game is the build the offline runs read (2.7.2.0, worldwide). If the launcher says the build is not
    supported, stop: the game has been updated since, and the offline run must be made again first.
  - Starts the game offline without Easy Anti-Cheat, by writing the game's Steam app id into steam_appid.txt beside
    the game while it starts, and removing it again (eldennet-launcher.exe --cleanup removes it if a crash left it).
  - Loads eldennet.dll into the game. In the game, EldenNet reads the game's code and data as they are loaded,
    read-only and through guarded copies, and finds again the 217 places the offline runs found. It also looks
    through the code for four other ways the same fetch of a manager is written (gal\recipes.toml lists them, each
    with its note); the third round counted them: 1,279 more places, which lead to the same managers and to two
    places more, which hold nothing EldenNet asks for.
  - For each of those places it reads the address kept there and, where there is one, the first eight bytes of the
    object at that address, once. Those eight bytes say what kind of object it is, and the game's own type
    information, already read from its code, gives that kind a class. Where the class is one of the eleven EldenNet
    asks for, the place is that manager's. For the world's clock, a kind whose name holds the expected one counts
    too, marked as weaker until a later round confirms it. It does this again every ten seconds, for as long as any
    of the eleven is still missing.
  - From a thread of its own, about ten times a second, it reads each named manager's place and the first eight bytes
    of its object again, and counts: how often the place was empty, how often the object was still of the same kind,
    how often a read was refused, and how long a reading took. This is the way EldenNet v1 will read the game, tried
    here on sixteen bytes a manager.
  - From the same thread, every two seconds, the watch: it follows the way to each named manager's object and
    copies its first 512 bytes (fewer where the object is smaller). From one copy to the next it counts, for every
    eight bytes, whether they changed and whether they rose, and now and then what they look like: nothing, a small
    number, a kind of object, an address in the game's code or data, an address of memory in use, or something
    else. It also counts, for every run of six bits, whether it behaves as a clock's minutes do (never 60 or more,
    stepping forward and going round), for every run of five whether it behaves as hours (never 24 or more,
    stepping by one), and for every four bytes whether, read as a fraction or as a whole number, they keep rising
    at a steady pace; and when such hours or such a number jump instead (a rise far above the pace, or a fall), it
    keeps the first three jumps: after how many seconds, and the number before and after. Beside the managers it
    watches: the objects the event flags' first addresses lead to (eight at most) and the objects those lead to
    (four each at most); the object where the play time is kept; 16,384 bytes of the object of the clock's kind and
    of the manager that points at it; and, once your save is loaded and every six seconds (less often where
    nothing ever changes), the object behind each of the other known places, for a clock only. It writes counts
    and offsets, and for a number that jumped the number before and after each of its first three jumps (the
    clock's own reading, if it is the clock): nothing else the memory held.
  - From the same thread, the sweep: from each address the event flags' own object keeps (eight at most) it copies
    up to 2 MiB of memory, and from each address one step further (32 at most) up to 256 KiB, a piece of 16,384
    bytes at a time, and compares each piece with the copy before. Where one single bit of a byte has changed, in
    eight bytes that otherwise keep still, it writes where (the offset from the address), which bit, whether it
    went up or down, and after how many seconds: never what the memory holds. A pass through all of it is followed
    by a rest, so that a pass starts every thirty seconds at most.
  - Once your save is loaded, from the same thread, three searches, each of which only reads:
      - For a manager whose kind of object the game has but which none of those places holds: it goes through the
        game's own data for another place that points at an object of that kind, reading the first eight bytes of
        each object it finds a pointer to, three times at most. Where several places point at one and the same
        object, the manager is named by the one the game's code refers to most.
      - For the same managers: it copies the first 16,384 bytes of the object behind each of the places, and looks
        there for an object of the missing kind, or for a pointer to one (eight bytes read behind each pointer).
        What it finds, the watch then follows.
      - For the play time: over the objects the game's data points at, it looks for a number that rises by the
        clock while you play. The place it finds goes into the table, with the places in the game's code that refer
        to it; the watch then follows the object, and this search ends.
    The searches and the watch follow pointers that may no longer be good. So the round first asks Windows, for
    every number in the game's data that could be an address, whether there is memory there that may be read
    (VirtualQuery, which reads nothing), and keeps only those; 96 in 100 of them are no address at all. Before each
    read it asks again, and leaves the memory alone if the answer has changed; then it copies with Windows' own
    function for reading a program's memory (ReadProcessMemory, on the game itself), which answers "cannot be read"
    without disturbing the game. Such a read takes about half a thousandth of a second on your PC, so the round
    looks at its clock after every one: together they take 4 thousandths of a second of each step of EldenNet's own
    thread, give or take one read; the game's own threads are never used. What they find is written as counts and
    places, never as what the memory held.
  - Every two seconds it writes lodestar-found.json into %APPDATA%\EldenNet\probe, and lodestar-table.toml when
    another manager is named. If the report ever grows past its limit, it is written without the detail of the
    watched object that has the most, then of the next, and says which were left out. They hold only numbers
    (offsets, sizes, counts, and the jumps' numbers named above) and EldenNet's own labels: no names of the game's,
    no text, no paths, nothing of your character or your PC.
  - Watches the game until it ends, and keeps a short record if it ends badly: the error's code, which module it was
    in and the time, never a memory dump.


What it never does
------------------

  - It never calls anything in the game, never adds anything to the game's own lists, and never changes the game's
    code or data. It reads them.
  - It never goes online, and the game cannot reach FromSoftware's servers without Easy Anti-Cheat.
  - It never changes your saves or the game's files (steam_appid.txt aside, which it removes).
  - It never runs anything else, uses the network, keeps keys or sends anything anywhere. You send the files yourself.


If the game crashes or freezes
------------------------------

  - Your saves were backed up before the game started: each backup is a folder in %APPDATA%\EldenNet\save-backups,
    named by date and time, with a manifest of the files' sizes and hashes. The game's own save is not touched by
    EldenNet; only if the game itself reports a damaged save, copy ER0000.sl2 (and ER0000.sl2.bak) from the newest
    backup back into %APPDATA%\EldenRing\<the same number>\, with the game closed.
  - If the game freezes, close it with Alt+F4, or end eldenring.exe in Task Manager.
  - Send the files of step 5, the newest crash record included. The next start of the launcher explains it once.
  - To play normally again, start the game from Steam as usual. Nothing of EldenNet stays loaded.


Check the files
---------------

In PowerShell, in this folder:

  Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 eldennet-launcher.exe, eldennet.dll, eldennet-engine.exe

Each value must equal its line in SHA256SUMS (letter case aside).


Safe use, and what we condemn
-----------------------------

The devkit's own words, as "eldennet-engine.exe probe-exe --help" prints them:

@DISCLOSURE@
